<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="redirect-and-wrap" title="Redirect and Wrap">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Both take over an operation the target performs. A redirect replaces it outright. A wrap
        hands your handler a handle to it. The handler decides whether it happens.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["A call, field access or instantiation"] --> B{"What claims it"}
    B -->|"@Redirect"| C["Your handler gets the operands"]
    B -->|"@Wrap"| D["Operands, and an Operation"]
    C --> G["Original never runs"]
    D --> E{"Was call invoked"}
    E -->|no| G
    E -->|yes| F["Next handler runs, or the original if innermost"]
    G --> H["What the handler returns stands in"]
    F --> H
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="Which one you want" id="choose">
        <p>The question is whether your handler needs the original.</p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Difference</td>
                <td><code>@Redirect</code></td>
                <td><code>@Wrap</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The handler receives</td>
                <td>the operands</td>
                <td>the operands and an <code>Operation</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The original</td>
                <td>is gone</td>
                <td>runs only at the innermost level</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Extra parameters</td>
                <td>target arguments and <code>@Local</code></td>
                <td>none</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Two on one site</td>
                <td>refused</td>
                <td>they nest</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Cost per execution</td>
                <td>one call</td>
                <td>one call and an array</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            <a href="handlers-and-callbacks.topic">Handlers and callbacks</a> has the bands those
            parameters are declared in. <a href="locals.topic">Locals</a> says what a redirect does
            with a capture. Both declarations also refuse a <code>shift</code>, which
            <a href="injection-points.topic">injection points</a> covers.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What counts as an operation" id="operations">
        <p>
            A handler stands in for the operation. So the operation needs a signature. Three
            instructions carry one. The engine reads it off whatever the point matched.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Operation</td>
                <td>The handler's leading parameters</td>
                <td>It returns</td>
            </tr>
            <tr><td>Instance call</td><td>the receiver, then the arguments</td>
                <td>the call's return type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Static call</td><td>the arguments</td><td>the call's return type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Instance field read</td><td>the owner</td><td>the field's type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Static field read</td><td>none</td><td>the field's type</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Instance field write</td><td>the owner, then the value</td>
                <td><code>void</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Static field write</td><td>the value</td><td><code>void</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Instantiation</td><td>the constructor's arguments</td>
                <td>the created type</td></tr>
        </table>

        <list>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A receiver is pushed before the descriptor's own arguments and is no part of
                    it. It is therefore the first parameter, and an instance operation has one
                    input more than its descriptor.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A matched instruction that is none of the three is <code>AW1061</code>: name
                    the call, field access or instantiation you meant, or use <code>@Inject</code>,
                    which adds code at a bare position.
                    <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> says which points can
                    reach an operation at all.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A point a plugin contributes is never checked by name. It is judged by the
                    instruction it lands on, and the same code follows.
                    <a href="plugins.topic">Extending the engine</a> covers contributing one.
                </p>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="An instantiation is one operation" id="new">
        <p>
            A <code>new</code> in a class file is an allocation and a constructor call. A
            <code>dup</code> sits between them when the result is kept, and both declarations take
            over all of it.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The allocation stops existing, and so does the <code>dup</code> when there is
                    one. Your handler produces the reference itself, and the call to it replaces
                    the constructor call.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Everything between them stays. That is the code computing the arguments your
                    handler is about to receive.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    No receiver is among the inputs. The object does not exist until your handler
                    returns one.
                </p>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The Operation a wrap receives" id="operation">
        <p>
            The engine writes the operation into the site as a dynamic constant. The JVM resolves
            that once and caches it. Every execution hands your handler the same instance.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="call(Object...)">
                <p>
                    Performs the next level in and returns what it produced. Call it twice and the
                    operation happens twice. Never call it, and it never happens: whatever your
                    handler returns stands in.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Its arguments">
                <p>
                    Exactly the operation's inputs, in the order the JVM pushes them, and you may
                    pass values other than the ones you were handed. A different number is an
                    <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> naming the operation and both counts. It
                    is thrown at run time; nothing counts them while the class is woven.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Boxing">
                <p>
                    Arguments and result cross as <code>Object</code>. A primitive is boxed both
                    ways, and an operation producing nothing yields <code>null</code>. A reference
                    of the wrong type fails with a <code>ClassCastException</code>, and a
                    <code>null</code> where the operation takes a primitive with a
                    <code>NullPointerException</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="What it throws">
                <p>
                    Whatever the operation throws passes through unchanged, checked exceptions
                    included. The target's own <code>catch</code> blocks see what they were
                    compiled against.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                The handle holds nothing from the target's frame. A handler that stores it in a
                field and calls it later performs the operation again, outside the target's control
                flow.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Several claims on one operation" id="nesting">
        <p>
            Wraps nest instead of colliding. Only the outermost is called from the site. The rest
            are bound into the <code>Operation</code> it receives.
        </p>

        <list>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The outermost is the first in plan order, which puts the highest
                    <code>@Weave(priority)</code> on the outside.
                    <a href="ordering.topic">Ordering</a> has the tie-breakers behind it.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    So <code>call</code> reaches the next handler in, not the target's own
                    instruction. Only the innermost reaches that.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    An inner handler that performs nothing returns a value of its own, and the one
                    outside it cannot tell.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    A redirect never joins the nest. Two redirects on one site, or a redirect
                    meeting a wrap, is <code>AW1060</code>: make both of them <code>@Wrap</code>,
                    or narrow one with an <code>ordinal</code> or a <code>slice</code>.
                </p>
                <deflist type="medium">
                    <def title="What it compares">
                        <p>
                            The site as the declaration spells it: the method text, the point, the
                            target, the ordinal and the slice — not the resolved instruction.
                        </p>
                    </def>
                    <def title="Its limit">
                        <p>
                            Two declarations reaching the same instruction through different
                            spelling collide silently. So does a bare <code>ordinal</code> meeting
                            one that pins it.
                        </p>
                    </def>
                    <def title="If it misses">
                        <p>
                            Nothing catches it at emission either. Both handlers replace the
                            instruction, and the class file ends up with two calls where the
                            operation stood.
                        </p>
                    </def>
                </deflist>
            </li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

